---
`yume`, `yumed` and the C ABI run native YTP/1 over one set of components.
Transport v2, the protocol before YTP/1, has been removed, and commit
`9070b0a` is the last that contains it.
[IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.md](IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.md) owns the open gates.

## Vocabulary

@table
| Term | Means |
| --- | --- |
| **YTP/1** | **YUME Transport Protocol 1.** The wire protocol `yume` and `yumed` speak. Version 1 of a new protocol, not "YUME 1". Its kernel is `src/ytp/` and its contract is [protocol/YTP_1.md](protocol/YTP_1.md). |
| **schema 1** | The strict configuration schema for both roles, parsed by `src/config/v1/`. |
| **ABI v1** | The role-neutral C interface in `include/yume/yume.h`. Its version is independent of every wire and product version. |
| **admission** | The cheap, replay-protected proof a client sends before its carrier is promoted. A connection without a valid proof gets the configured cover and never reaches AUTH. |
| **admission key** | The 32-byte key the server and its clients share for admission. It must differ from every access PSK. |
| **access PSK** | The 32-byte secret one authorized client identity shares with the server. It enters the YTP/1 key schedule. |
| **cover** | The website a visitor without a valid admission proof sees. `yumed` serves a static site it loads at start. There is no built-in fallback site, and a configured reverse-proxy cover is refused as not implemented yet. |
| **carrier** | The outer connection YTP records ride inside: TLS 1.3, then HTTP/2 with an RFC 8441 extended CONNECT stream of WebSocket binary frames. |
| **relay channel** | The end-to-end channel between two users that the relay module library keeps for the planned chat and file modules. Its labels come from transport v2. See [protocol/RELAY_CHANNEL.md](protocol/RELAY_CHANNEL.md). |
| **BaseFWX** | A separate pinned repository of cryptographic primitives, checked out at `basefwx/`. Only the module libraries build on it. `yume`, `yumed` and `libyume` do not link it. |
| **evidence profile** | A captured browser identity such as `chrome151-node24-v1`. It describes geometry only. It is not a wire version, and recapturing one does not change the protocol. |
@end

The product (`0.3.0-dev1`), YTP/1, schema 1, ABI v1, the evidence profile and
the relay channel protocol (version 2) are **independent version axes**. Do
not bump one to match another.

## Directories

Implementation paths below are relative to `src/`. The candidate header in
`include/yume/` is relative to the repository root.

@table
| Path | Owns |
| --- | --- |
| `engine/` | Dependency-clean contracts and the session: `ByteChannel`, `SecureChannel`, `Carrier`, `FrontDoor`, `RouteProvider`, `StreamHandler`, `SessionEngine` and `SessionBootstrap` |
| `ytp/` | YTP/1 protocol kernel and security domains. Protocol and AUTH/key-schedule codecs share internal big-endian operations in `ytp/detail/byte_order.hpp`, and each codec owns its bounds checks and error order. It may not include the engine |
| `providers/` | OpenSSL session security, internal deterministic `ytp1_crypto` constructions, TLS channels, Asio TCP/UDP routes and client/accepted TCP channels, the H2 carrier, the native FrontDoor and configured static cover. `loopback_http_fetch.*` fetches GET and HEAD from a loopback web server for a reverse-proxy cover, and `socks5_upstream.*` reaches a server through a SOCKS5 proxy. `child_process.*` starts a helper program with one passed descriptor and watches it through a pidfd, and `process_privileges.*` drops a helper's privileges before it works |
| `runtime/` | `native_credentials.*` loads protected schema-1 files and per-identity service policy. `native_endpoint.*` owns native provider/bootstrap/session composition and optional automatic server accepts, paced by the dependency-clean `accept_scheduler.hpp`. `native_egress_policy.*` evaluates configured direct-adapter destinations and their egress lists, which `egress_lists.*` reads, with MaxMind country databases, into sorted address ranges. `local_listener.*` owns the loopback TCP and private UNIX socket accept loop that `native_socks5.*` and `native_forward.*` share. `egress_limiter.*` shares a server's configured egress rate between identities by weight, and `paced_stream.*` applies it to each stream the endpoint serves. `module_supervisor.*` runs each configured module program, restarts it and hands it its service's streams over a private UNIX socket, and `module_launcher.*` is the `yume-module` entry that drops privileges before running the program. `native_server_runtime.*`, `native_client_runtime.*`, `native_socks5.*`, `native_socks5_udp.*` and `native_cli.cpp` build the `yumed` and `yume` processes |
| `abi/` | The opt-in C ABI. `yume_c.cpp` owns handles, input validation, diagnostics, callback rules and backend leasing. It accepts only schema-1 documents, parses them through `config/v1` and reaches the runtime only through the embedding seam `endpoint_backend.hpp`. `native_backend.cpp` is `yume_embed`, which runs `NativeEndpoint` on its own execution thread and links no BaseFWX. `compatibility_manifest.hpp` is the version view the ABI reports |
| `common/` | Dependency-clean contracts shared across layers and the ABI: the canonical service-name grammar and bound, canonical destination networks, the egress address classes that configuration and native policy share, the UDP datagram budget the SOCKS5 UDP relay uses, hex encoding, best-effort secret erasure, the development timing helpers, and the product and protocol version constants |
| `admission/` | Shared path/authority parsing, private-context HMAC and bounded monotonic replay reservations, with no protocol or runtime dependency |
| `stealth/` | The browser-shaped outer layers: the Chrome TLS client profile applied to OpenSSL, the active cover profile with its generated registry, TLS fingerprints, and the HTTP/2 and WebSocket carrier with its wire profile and outer observer. `clienthello_dump.cpp` and `h2_opening_probe.cpp` are evidence probes that the tests run |
| `fs/` | Bounded regular-file reads, and reading and writing owner-only secret files. Reads open nonblocking and refuse special files before reading |
| `config/v1/` | The strict schema-1 parser with RFC 6901 error pointers |
| `modules/` | Module programs, which link no YUME library, and the libraries they build on. `echo/` is the example module that the tests run. `relay/` is the relay channel between two users (handshake, records and hybrid ratchet), the stores its users keep (encrypted chat history, the password-derived relay secret and peer trust), and the invite and directory records with the policies that check them. `files/` holds relay file transfer on top of it: an outbound source pinned to one opened file and a confined receiver that publishes only a verified file. `codecs/` is the application codec core for a planned codec module: the HTTP request and response envelope, backend dialing rules and the Monero RPC descriptor. `share/` is the password-sealed `.yss` container for a planned kit import format. The libraries build only with `YUME_BUILD_BASEFWX_MODULES=ON` |
| `gui/` | The desktop application written for transport v2. It stays as source for the GUI phase and does not build: `YUME_BUILD_GUI=ON` stops configuration with a message |
| `test_support/` | Allocation-failure hooks and generated TLS identities for isolated tests, with no production allocator or runtime dependency |
| `include/yume/` | The public candidate C header. It is unfrozen, and development SDK installation is opt-in |
@end

## Composition and open gaps

The native endpoint composes every component below. Follow actual target
inputs, not file names:

@table
| Boundary | Current source evidence | Still open |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Server ingress | `providers/h2_web_front_door.*` implements native listening, configured static cover, exporter/replay admission and retained H2/TLS promotion | Composed through `runtime/native_endpoint.*` by `yumed` and the schema-1 ABI backend. Qualification remains |
| Client admission | `providers/h2_duplex_carrier.*` owns a 32-byte key and emits a fresh exporter-bound proof through `ytp1_h2_admission.*` | Composed by `runtime/native_endpoint.*` and reached through the schema-1 ABI backend and `yume` |
| TLS profile | `providers/tls13_secure_channel.cpp` applies `stealth/tls_client_profile.*`, and a published YTP channel still requires TLS 1.3 and H2 | Qualify actual provider output and full-session behavior. Profile configuration alone does not establish circumvention |
| Session composition | `runtime/native_endpoint.cpp` calls `SessionBootstrap::create` for both roles, retains active engines and composes configured direct TCP/UDP adapters whose schema-1 destinations `runtime/native_egress_policy.*` enforces | `abi/native_backend.cpp` drives named streams/packets and routed client OPENs for the ABI. Schema-1 adapters are refused there, because they are standalone application components |
| Embedding | `abi/yume_c.cpp` accepts schema-1 documents only and runs the native backend, which carries named byte streams, named packets and destination-routed TCP/UDP OPENs | Supported-platform SDK and production qualification |
| Applications | `yumed` and `yume` run direct routes, forwards, SOCKS5 CONNECT/UDP ASSOCIATE, modules and Linux managed TUN over `NativeEndpoint` | Packaging, the GUI and Android phases, and production qualification |
@end

`yume_h2_carrier` is the single build owner of the `stealth/` H2,
WebSocket, wire-profile and observer sources, and the native adapter
`yume_h2_duplex_carrier` links it. It propagates the diagnostics setting to
callers because public declarations depend on it. Release and MinSizeRel
compile those diagnostics out. `yume_cover_profile` owns profile data and
fingerprint interpretation, and `yume_tls_client_profile` owns SSL_CTX profile
configuration. The H2 library links profile data, nghttp2, OpenSSL Crypto and
platform threading, with no SSL, BaseFWX or session-engine dependency.
`yume_bounded_files` owns the bounded and confined reader that the cover site,
the egress lists and the share module use. Every archive the shared ABI takes
in, these two included, gets hidden, PIC, non-LTO preparation first.
`yume_native_endpoint` links `yume_direct_route_handler` directly for
configured destination adapters. That archive depends only on the engine
contracts.

`admission/h2_admission.*` owns shared path/authority parsing, HMAC and
monotonic replay reservations. It links only OpenSSL Crypto and Boost headers,
and layering rejects dependencies on either protocol or runtime.
`providers/ytp1_h2_admission.*` owns the distinct exporter-bound
[YTP admission construction](protocol/YTP_1.md#h2-admission-v1). It deliberately
omits wall time and evidence-profile IDs. `providers/h2_web_front_door.*`
combines those helpers with received TLS SNI, a locally derived exporter, a
caller-shared replay cache, configured cover and one promotion per TLS lifetime.

`providers/cover_site.*` loads an immutable operator-supplied static site
through `runtime::FileRoot`, including an index and explicit not-found file.
Requests select preloaded routes without filesystem access or DNS. There is
no built-in fallback site or reverse-proxy implementation yet.
The public TLS entry accepts ordinary TLS 1.2/1.3 and HTTP/1.1/H2 cover traffic,
and only TLS 1.3 with H2 can become a YTP `SecureChannel`.

`AsioTcpAcceptedChannelOwner` adopts already-connected TCP or UNIX stream
sockets using the same bounded channel state and registry as client TCP. It
supplies no listener or cover policy. The FrontDoor uses it for every accepted
socket, settles its output and timer before transfer, and passes the same TLS
and H2 objects to the carrier. Cover streams whose request side remains open
transfer with their existing parser and cover ledger. Published carriers
retain the accepted-channel owner and configured cover independently of the
listener's lifetime. `AsioExecutionContext` owns the concrete single-runner
execution and reserved control dispatch used by ingress TCP, direct TCP/UDP
routes and promoted H2 carriers. `yume_asio_execution_context` owns that shared
implementation, and the two Asio socket-provider targets link it
independently. The shared `providers/control_task.hpp` mailbox retains
embedded task owners without allocation and adds no Asio dependency to H2 or
the engine. See the [ByteChannel lifecycle](ARCHITECTURE.md#bytechannel) for
initiation and drain requirements. The FrontDoor binds H2 dispatch to its
context. Its caller must contain runner exceptions, close owners, call
`finish()` and drain completions. Ingress performs no resolution.
Qualification must exercise sustained allocation failure as well as ordinary
cancellation.

`providers/system_resolver.*` owns name lookup for both Asio network
providers through a killable helper process, described in the
[ByteChannel lifecycle](ARCHITECTURE.md#bytechannel). It starts the helper
through `providers/child_process.*`, as the module supervisor starts modules.
`providers/system_resolver_protocol.hpp` is the private message format and
`providers/system_resolver_helper.*` the helper side, which links only libc
and threads. The standalone `yume-resolver` program
(`providers/system_resolver_main.cpp`) serves embedding hosts. `yume` and
`yumed` dispatch to the same helper when started under that name. The test
helper `yume_resolver_stall_helper` adds one name that never resolves.

`EndpointBackend` has one endpoint interface, and its service operations are
directional: a server registers and accepts, and a client opens. YTP's engine
can open services in either role. The schema-1 backend keeps the ABI direction
because a server-initiated open needs an authenticated way to choose one of the
server's sessions, which the endpoint API does not offer. A schema-1 client
therefore refuses OPENs from its server. This is an ABI decision, not a
protocol rule.

### Data through the components

The native endpoint composes the following path. The schema-1 ABI backend
supplies named byte streams to it, and the SOCKS5 adapter supplies CONNECT
byte streams and UDP ASSOCIATE packets. `runtime/native_packet_adapter.*`
bridges a leased TUN through directional IP policy,
`runtime/linux_tun_network.*` owns Linux addresses, routes and DNS, and
`providers/linux_tun_packet_channel.*` owns asynchronous device I/O.

@table
| Stage | Data and operation | Source owner |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Application to session | A named service opens a byte stream or packet channel. Writes consume stream and connection credit, then become canonical DATA or PACKET frames. | `engine/session_engine.cpp`, `ytp/protocol.cpp` |
| Record protection | The security provider seals the canonical frame with AES-256-GCM. A directional epoch and sequence identify a one-use key/nonce pair. The engine prefixes the ciphertext with that token. | `engine/session_engine.cpp::protect_frame`, `providers/openssl_security_provider.cpp` |
| H2 carrier | A 12-byte length envelope preserves each record across partial reads. The carrier sends it in WebSocket binary frames on an RFC 8441 extended CONNECT stream and accounts for receive credit. | `providers/h2_duplex_carrier.cpp`, `stealth/h2_carrier.cpp`, `stealth/websocket_codec.cpp` |
| Outer connection | OpenSSL TLS 1.3 encrypts H2 bytes. Memory BIOs exchange encrypted bytes with a ByteChannel. The Asio client provider reads and writes the TCP socket. | `providers/tls13_secure_channel.cpp`, `providers/asio_tcp_byte_channel_provider.cpp` |
| Peer delivery | The reverse path removes TLS and carrier framing, verifies the record token/tag, decodes the canonical frame and dispatches its stream ID. Invalid protection or framing terminates the session. | TLS/H2 providers, `engine/session_engine.cpp` |
| Destination access | OPEN authorization produces an `AuthorizedRouteRequest`. The Asio provider checks every selected numeric address through an explicit policy before socket creation. The direct-route handler bridges the stream to a TCP or UDP route, retains credit until delivery and closes both sides on terminal failure. | `engine/session_engine.cpp::process_open`, `providers/direct_route_handler.cpp`, `providers/asio_direct_route_provider.cpp` |
@end

The egress endpoint decrypts YUME records and can read their contents. An
application's own HTTPS remains application data inside the tunnel and keeps
its separate browser-to-origin TLS protection. Reply traffic uses the opposite
directional record keys and independent credit accounting.

`yume_private_files` owns `fs/secret_file.cpp`. The native credential loader
and the module libraries link it, and it links no BaseFWX.

### Authentication and cryptographic ownership

TLS server authentication, carrier admission, YTP peer authentication and
service authorization are separate checks. The native FrontDoor verifies
admission, `runtime/native_credentials.*` loads protected schema-1 credentials
and per-identity authorization, and `runtime/native_endpoint.*` composes
sessions.

@table
| Mechanism | Purpose and implementation |
| --- | --- |
| TLS 1.3 | `tls13_secure_channel.cpp` verifies the configured trust and hostname and selects H2 with ALPN. Distinct TLS exporter labels bind YTP authentication and H2 admission to this connection. Its client applies the browser profile. Full-session stealth remains unqualified. |
| Ed25519 + ML-DSA-87 | `openssl_security_provider.cpp::verify_composite` requires both signatures for the composite identity. The client checks its configured server identity. The server selects an authorized client identity and its access PSK. |
| SHA-256 and HMAC-SHA256 | SHA-256 hashes canonical transcript/identity inputs. HMAC authenticates the per-identity PSK proof and key confirmation. Hashing alone provides no confidentiality. The admission key and access PSK have different roles. |
| X25519 + ML-KEM-1024 | Hybrid establishment contributes both shared secrets. `derive_initial_roots` binds them with the access PSK, TLS exporter, transcript, roles, identities and capabilities through the canonical key-schedule encoding. |
| HKDF-SHA256 and AES-256-GCM | HKDF derives directional roots and per-record key/nonce material. GCM encrypts and authenticates application frames. Suite, direction, epoch and sequence enter the authenticated data. Rekey uses its own domain and authenticated exchange. |
| Primitive library | The YTP session-security provider uses a private OpenSSL 3.5 context and its default provider. Its target links OpenSSL Crypto, engine and YTP, with no BaseFWX or liboqs dependency. The relay module library takes ML-KEM-1024, X25519, HKDF and AES-GCM from BaseFWX and its signatures from OpenSSL. |
@end

Exact encodings, domain strings, AUTH flights and the special REKEY_ACK
protection rule belong to [YTP/1](protocol/YTP_1.md). The algorithms and focused
tests do not establish complete-session stealth, independent cryptographic
review or measured performance.

## Layering, and where it is enforced

@code text
engine + ytp1 -> session_engine -> session_bootstrap
engine -> providers (security, TLS, H2 carrier, FrontDoor, routes, resolver)
config_v1 + session_bootstrap + providers -> native_endpoint
native_endpoint -> native_runtime -> yume, yumed
native_endpoint -> embed -> abi (libyume)
basefwx -> modules (relay, files, share)
@end

[`cmake/YumeLayering.cmake`](../cmake/YumeLayering.cmake) defines two checks
that run during configuration. CTest `yume_03_layering_check` reruns the source
include check, without reconstructing the target link graph.

`yume_assert_exact_link_dependencies` pins a target's exact direct link list.
Every library in `src/CMakeLists.txt` carries one, from the engine and YTP/1
up to `yume_native_runtime`, `yume_embed`, `yume_abi` and the module
libraries.

`yume_check_03_source_layering` reads sources and rejects forbidden includes.
`engine/`, `ytp/` and `common/` may not touch Boost, Asio, OpenSSL, nghttp2,
JSON, `<filesystem>` or sockets. `ytp/` may not include the engine. Inside the
engine only `session_engine.cpp` and tests include YTP/1, and nothing includes
`config/`. `common/` includes no other layer, and `fs/`, `stealth/` and
`admission/` build only on it. `providers/` may not include `runtime/`,
`config/`, `abi/`, `gui/`, `modules/` or BaseFWX. `runtime/` may not include
`abi/`, `gui/`, `modules/` or BaseFWX, and `abi/` may not include `gui/`,
`modules/` or BaseFWX.

Both checks fail at configure time. A `gui/` include in `abi/`, or a
`providers/` include in `stealth/`, stops the build rather than being noticed
later.

## Where to look

@table
| Task | Start at |
| --- | --- |
| Wire format, frame flags | `ytp/protocol.hpp`, [YTP/1](protocol/YTP_1.md) |
| Handshake, record protection, rekey | `ytp/security.hpp`, `providers/openssl_security_provider.cpp`, `engine/session_engine.cpp` |
| Browser-shaped TLS/H2 | `stealth/`, `providers/tls13_secure_channel.cpp`, `providers/h2_duplex_carrier.cpp` |
| Front door and cover | `providers/h2_web_front_door.cpp`, `providers/cover_site.cpp` |
| Starting `yume` and `yumed` | `runtime/native_cli.cpp`, `runtime/native_client_runtime.cpp`, `runtime/native_server_runtime.cpp` |
| Egress policy | `runtime/native_egress_policy.cpp`, `runtime/egress_lists.cpp`, `providers/asio_direct_route_provider.cpp` |
| SOCKS5 and forwards | `runtime/native_socks5.cpp`, `runtime/native_socks5_udp.cpp`, `runtime/native_forward.cpp` |
| Managed TUN | `runtime/native_packet_adapter.cpp`, `runtime/linux_tun_network.cpp`, `providers/linux_tun_packet_channel.cpp` |
| Modules | `runtime/module_supervisor.cpp`, `runtime/module_launcher.cpp`, [MODULES.md](MODULES.md) |
| Embedding YUME | `include/yume/yume.h`, [ABI.md](ABI.md), `abi/stream_probe.c` |
| Adding a transport backend | `abi/endpoint_backend.hpp` |
| Configuration keys | `config/v1/config.hpp`, `config/v1/config.cpp` |
| Kits and checks | `tools/yume_setup.py`, `tools/yume_doctor.py` |
@end

## Tests

C++ unit tests sit next to their source as `*_test.cpp` and are registered
individually in `src/CMakeLists.txt`. Integration and system tests live in
`tests/`. The end-to-end ABI data paths are `yume_abi_stream_integration`,
`yume_abi_packet_integration` and `yume_abi_route_integration`. Each
provisions a real server and client and moves data over a named stream, a
named packet channel or a routed destination.

## Documentation tooling

`docs/src/en_US/` owns documentation text and publication metadata, and
`scripts/yume_doc_spec.py` parses it once for Markdown, roff and web renderers.
`scripts/yume_docs.py` collects and validates all outputs before replacing
changed files. `docs/diagrams/*.json` owns figure topology and labels, while
`@diagram` in a document owns placement. Website colors and fonts also drive
standalone SVGs. See [the authoring guide](src/README.md).
